---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/fashion-design/foundational/fashion-design-research">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the first lesson of Fashion Design, and it deliberately starts before any
    drawing. You need nothing from earlier lessons — only attention to the clothes and
    images around you and a willingness to look slowly. Everything else is built here,
    step by step.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain what fashion design research is, and why a collection begins long before the first sketch;</li>
    <li>build and curate a research book — the difference between primary and secondary material, and why the book is an argument rather than an archive;</li>
    <li>develop a concept: compress gathered material into a one-sentence controlling idea, and transform references into structure instead of copying them;</li>
    <li>plan a line-up as an ensemble — statement pieces, core pieces and commercial closes that talk to each other;</li>
    <li>explain why fabric is the design's co-author, and why the fabric story is fixed during research;</li>
    <li>use design history and subculture as a reference library, so the present's revivals and remixes have named sources.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit: gather with intent. Research is not collecting everything; it is deciding
    what evidence your idea needs, then collecting that. Scientists plan their data
    collection before an experiment for exactly the same reason — a blueprint for gathering
    evidence, drawn up before the conclusions — and designers who borrow that discipline
    never end up with an empty pantry.
  </p>

  <h2>Nobody opens a restaurant by writing the menu first</h2>
  <p>
    Fashion design begins before sketching: with research. A collection is an argument
    about the world — a mood, a silhouette, a palette — and the designer's first job is to
    gather, curate and transform the raw material that argument needs. Think of it like
    cooking: nobody opens a restaurant by writing the menu first. They taste, shop, and
    find what the season offers; the menu is the last, shortest step. Designers who skip
    research are writing menus from an empty pantry, and the result is the same in both
    trades — generic dishes that taste of nothing in particular.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="bet before you read on — the day-one sketcher" widget="predict" config={fdrMenuPredict} />
  <p>
    Research also answers a question every designer eventually faces: why should this
    collection exist? Clothes are made by the millions every season, and most are
    forgettable. The collections that matter come from a designer who has looked hard at
    something — a subculture, a historical decade, a material behaviour — and has something
    to say about it. Research is how a designer finds that something to say.
  </p>
  <p>
    The research book is the industry's most revealing artefact. Show a designer's book and
    you can predict the collection: the mood pages become the palette, the texture pages
    become the fabric list, the structural studies become the seams. This is why tutors,
    juries and design directors read books so closely — the book shows the thinking, and
    thinking is what they are hiring. A sketch on its own is an opinion; a research book is
    the evidence behind it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> research is the phase where a designer finds something worth
    saying and gathers the evidence for saying it. The pipeline below compresses the whole
    journey into five moves — read it now, then we take each move in turn.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from reference to collection: the research pipeline" widget="equationStep" config={researchCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the five stages are ordered, and the order is the
    teaching. Gathering comes before judging (stage 2), transformation comes before fabric
    decisions (stage 3 before stage 4), and the line-up is the last consideration, not the
    first (stage 5) — collections are designed from the inside out, not assembled from
    pretty sketches. When a project goes wrong, the failure can almost always be traced to
    the stage that was skipped.
  </p>

  <h2>Research books: gathering and curating</h2>
  <p>
    A research book (sometimes called a sketchbook, journal or workbook) is the designer's
    primary working document. It is part scrapbook, part laboratory notebook: photographs,
    fabric swatches, colour chips, postcards, magazine tears, pressed leaves, own drawings
    and handwritten notes, arranged so that a stranger could follow the argument. Books can
    be physical — preferred at most design schools, because fabric handles and colour read
    truer in person — or digital boards, but the discipline is identical.
  </p>
  <p>
    The most important distinction in gathering is <strong>primary versus secondary</strong>
    research. Primary research is material you generated or experienced yourself:
    photographs you took at a vintage market, rubbings from a manhole cover, a scarf you
    bought and unravelled to see how it was knitted, notes from an archive visit. Secondary
    research is material someone else made: images from books and museums, historical
    plates, film stills, online archives. Both are legitimate, but primary research is
    prized because it is unrepeatable — no other designer in the world has your photographs,
    your swatches, your observations. A book of only secondary images reads like a Pinterest
    board; a book with primary material reads like a designer.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="file each find: made by you, or made by someone else" widget="sort-buckets" config={fdrPrimaryBuckets} />
  <p>
    Curation is the second half of the skill. A strong book is an <em>argument, not an
    archive</em>: every image earns its place by supporting the developing idea. In
    practice this means editing ruthlessly — a degree-level book for one project typically
    runs tens of pages, not hundreds, and each spread is composed deliberately, like a
    magazine editor composes a story. Alongside the book, designers build
    <strong>moodboards</strong>: single compressed surfaces holding the palette, key
    textures, silhouette references and atmosphere words, so the whole mood can be absorbed
    in one glance. The moodboard is the collection's contract with itself — when a sketch
    later drifts off-mood, the board is what pulls it back.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="the argument and its contract — drag between them" widget="compare" config={fdrBookCompare} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> more images means more research. In reality, thirty
  images that are all responding to each other beat three hundred collected
  indiscriminately. Research is judged by depth of engagement — drawing over a photograph,
  testing a colour, taking a garment apart — not by volume of clippings.</p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="true or false — the volume fallacy" widget="judge" config={fdrVolumeJudge} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the book gathers raw material, privileges first-hand evidence,
    and edits it into an argument. The next question is what that argument is about — the
    concept.
  </p>

  <h2>Concept development: the transformation is the design</h2>
  <p>
    Concept development is the step where gathered material becomes a controlling idea. A
    <strong>concept</strong> is the collection's thesis, and it should be sayable in one
    sentence: "workwear deconstructed for the city", "a wardrobe for a future where water is
    rationed", "Edwardian mourning dress rebuilt in technical knit". The test of a good
    concept is that it keeps making decisions for you — it suggests silhouettes, excludes
    certain colours, points at particular fabrics. When a designer cannot say their concept
    in a sentence, every later decision becomes an argument with themselves.
  </p>
  <p>
    The heart of concept development is <strong>transformation</strong>: copying a reference
    is illustration; abstracting one is design. The discipline's classic exercise is to take
    a non-fashion image — a beetle's shell, a brutalist stairwell, a 1920s photogram — and
    translate it into structure, seam and surface. Concretely: a photograph of overlapping
    chapel windows might become a bodice whose panels overlap without meeting, the leading
    becoming piping, the coloured glass becoming a shaded colour gradient across the panels.
    The window is never printed on the cloth; it is rebuilt as construction. Judges and
    tutors read the distance between reference and outcome — too close is pastiche, too far
    is incoherence — and the sweet spot in between is where a designer's handwriting
    appears.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="put the design on the distance dial" widget="place" config={fdrDistancePlace} />
  <p>
    Concept development also means designing the <strong>line-up</strong>, not just
    individual garments. A professional women's or menswear collection is an ensemble:
    shapes that talk to each other, a deliberate balance of statement pieces and commercial
    ones. A typical twelve-look student capsule might hold two or three statement looks that
    carry the concept at full volume, six or seven core looks where the idea is worn daily,
    and a couple of commercial closes that a store could actually stock. Research is where
    this coherence is born — the mood board fixes the palette, the fabric board fixes the
    materials, and every look is checked against both.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="three checks that make a line-up an ensemble" widget="order" config={fdrChecksOrder} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> staying "faithful" to the reference by copying it
  closely is a virtue. It is the opposite — visible copying is the one failure every
  critique names first. The reference is fuel, not a template; the design is what you do
  to it.</p>

  <h2>Fabric is the design's co-author</h2>
  <p>
    Silhouettes are negotiations with material. A liquid satin wants the bias cut, where
    the cloth falls diagonally across its grain and drapes like poured water; a stiff
    duchess satin builds architecture, holding a sculpted shoulder or a bell-shaped skirt
    on its own; a technical knit shapes itself, stretching over the body without a single
    dart. The same sketch executed in three fabrics is three different garments — which is
    why professional practice is <em>drape-first</em>: pin cloth on the stand early, let its
    behaviour edit the sketch, and treat the fabric board as half the collection's identity.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="pair each cloth with what it wants to become" widget="match" config={fdrFabricMatch} />
  <p>
    The fabric story is locked during research, well before final sketches. Designers
    gather swatches — small samples of every candidate cloth — and sort them by hand-feel,
    weight, stretch and how they take colour. Students who sketch without swatches design
    garments that cannot exist: a collar that no available cloth will hold, a pleat that a
    heavy coating would crack at the fold.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> fabric is decoration, chosen at the end to "finish" a
  finished design. In practice the material decides what the silhouette can be — the bias
  drape, the sculpted shoulder and the stretch shape above are three different garments
  before any styling happens. Fabric is not the last step; it is the physical argument the
  whole collection is made of, and it is chosen while the research is still open.</p>

  <h2>Design history and reference</h2>
  <p>
    Every silhouette returns. The 1920s dropped the waist and shortened the skirt to
    liberate movement; Dior's 1947 New Look cinched the waist and poured out the skirt as a
    deliberate return to opulence after wartime rationing; the 1990s minimalists stripped
    everything back to the slip dress and the bias cut. Because the archive cycles, the
    designer who knows it knows what the present is quoting. A designer looking at today's
    runways without historical knowledge sees novelty everywhere; one who knows the century
    sees revivals, reactions and remixes with named sources.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="one century, three returns — set the chronology" widget="order" config={fdrHistoryOrder} />
  <p>
    Subcultures are the other great reference library. Punk's safety pins and DIY
    deconstruction travelled from 1970s streets to Vivienne Westwood's runways and then
    into mainstream leather jackets forever; hip-hop's oversized silhouettes, sportswear and
    logos reshaped menswear entirely. Designers research subculture because it is where
    clothing meaning is invented before the industry borrows it — and because the
    borrowings have histories worth knowing before repeating.
  </p>
  <p>
    The practical payoff is precision. Research into fashion history, subculture and the
    global industry is not academic decoration; it is the difference between proposing
    something new and unknowingly reprising something old. A graduate presenting "puffed
    shoulders and nipped waists" as a fresh idea will meet a jury member who quietly notes
    it is 1984. The same student who knows the 1984 power suit, knows why it meant authority
    then, and can articulate what it should mean now has turned reference into authorship.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "everything has already been done, so history means
  nothing is new." The useful reading is the reverse: because silhouettes return, novelty
  lives in the <em>reasoning</em> — new fabrics, new contexts, new wearers for old shapes.
  History is the vocabulary the present speaks with an accent.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> a collection grows from a curated book, through a one-sentence
    concept, into a line-up negotiated with real fabrics and placed inside a century of
    returning silhouettes. What remains is to see how the profession runs this process at
    speed — and how the book is judged.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>What is the difference between primary and secondary research, and why do juries weight the primary kind more heavily?</li>
    <li>A student's reference is a photograph of overlapping chapel windows. Describe one transformation of it into construction — and say why printing the photograph on the cloth would fail the exercise.</li>
    <li>Why is a twelve-look line-up planned as statement, core and commercial pieces rather than twelve independent designs?</li>
    <li>Your concept sentence is "a wardrobe for a future where water is rationed". Name one colour it probably excludes and one fabric it probably demands — that is the concept doing its job.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Primary material is generated by you (photographs,
    rubbings, unravelled garments); secondary is made by others (books, archives, film
    stills). Primary is unrepeatable — nobody else has it — so it produces a book that
    reads like a designer rather than a shared board. (2) For example: the window leading
    becomes piping between overlapping bodice panels that never meet; the coloured glass
    becomes a graded dye across the panels. Printing the image would copy the reference's
    surface instead of translating its structure — the transformation is the design. (3)
    Because a collection is an ensemble: statement looks carry the concept at full volume,
    core looks make it wearable, commercial closes make it stockable — coherence is the
    product being judged. (4) Any honest answer works: probably excludes saturated prints
    or crisp white luxury; probably demands hard-wearing, washable, low-impact cloth such
    as hemp or recycled fibre. If your answers surprised you, the concept is working.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Research precedes sketching: a collection is an argument, and research gathers its evidence.</li>
    <li>Primary research (your own photographs, swatches, observations) is prized because it is unrepeatable; secondary research supplies context.</li>
    <li>A research book is an argument, not an archive — curated, edited, and compressed into a moodboard that acts as the collection's contract.</li>
    <li>A concept is a one-sentence thesis that makes decisions for you; transformation of references — not copying — is where design happens.</li>
    <li>Fabric is the design's co-author: the same sketch in three cloths is three garments, so the fabric story is locked during research.</li>
    <li>Silhouettes return; history and subculture are reference libraries, and novelty lives in the reasoning, not the shape alone.</li>
    <li>A line-up is an ensemble: statement, core and commercial pieces that talk to each other.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    With the concept and evidence in place, the next lesson — Fashion illustration and
    flats — turns the research book's energy into communication: fashion drawing, technical
    flats and the portfolio pages that carry a line-up to a jury or a client. Later, the
    textiles unit returns to fabric with full depth, and the advanced collection unit
    scales the line-up thinking you met here to the runway.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    In industry, research operates inside a calendar and a brief. Collections are designed
    twelve to eighteen months ahead of delivery, so "research into the present" is partly
    systematised through trend agencies such as WGSN and colour systems such as Pantone,
    which publish season-ahead forecasts that much of the industry then reads — an odd loop
    where prediction shapes the future it predicts. Designer education deliberately breaks
    this loop: school projects usually write their own briefs from primary research
    precisely so students learn to generate ideas the forecasts did not hand them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Research books are also assessed as craft objects, with conventions worth learning
    early. Juries look for a visible journey: early pages broad and exploratory, middle
    pages narrowing through drawing and development, final pages resolving into flats,
    fabric boards and a colour story. Annotation matters because it externalises judgement
    — a note like "this drape flattens the silhouette; try the crisper poplin" shows
    decision-making, which is the thing being graded. Photocollage, traced silhouettes over
    references, fabric-manipulation samples taped to the page and colour chips pulled from
    photographs are all standard research verbs; pasting alone is not.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two further layers deepen professional practice. First, <strong>user and market
    research</strong>: commercial design adds who-wears-this analysis — price points, size
    ranges, care expectations — so the line-up's balance of statement and commercial pieces
    is evidence-based rather than guessed. Second, <strong>ethical sourcing as
    research</strong>: interrogating where a fabric comes from, what a print's cultural
    symbols mean to their originators, and whether deadstock or zero-waste construction
    could carry the concept is now core research, not an add-on — several of the most-cited
    graduate collections of recent years were built on the question "what if this collection
    generated no cutting-room waste?" The research book, in other words, is where a
    designer decides what kind of designer to be.
  </p>
  <p>
    A note on the book's afterlife: research does not end at the line-up. The best books
    are revisited — pages return as source material for later collections, colour stories
    get re-tested against new fabrics, and a designer's own archive becomes their private
    museum. Treat every project's research as an investment in the next ten years of
    authorship, not a disposable worksheet for one grade.
  </p>

